{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Phase 1 legitimacy checkpoint (rebar-debug): confirmed the reported mechanism locally by setting `rebar._store.git_locking.fcntl = None`; entering `_store_git_op_lock` and `fetch_coordination_lock` both raises `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'flock'`, matching CI run 33426859481 (`git_locking.py:224` via `_with_index_lock_retry`). However, the repository currently contains an authoritative contrary scope statement in `scripts/check_fcntl_import_guard.py:19-20`: “actually *using* `fcntl` off POSIX is out of scope — Windows is not a declared support target — only that import/collection succeeds.” The commit that added the import guard (5697f2e11179 / ticket 0b31-aeb5-e734-41c9) says the same: “Collectability only; making fcntl locking work on Windows stays out of scope (not a declared support target).” Per the reporter’s explicit guard, this makes the no-fcntl runtime-lock repair a design/support-scope decision rather than an auto-fixable defect. I am not changing production locking code or using force; escalation required to decide whether Windows runtime lock usage is now supported."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788218332610351206,"uuid":"e920f7b0-ce3f-4410-99ef-cfc380a09e2c"}